Register.

Every `# nosec` in this repository appears below, with the question that was
asked and the answer that was given. No pragma goes in unjustified, so an entry
here is the justification and not a record that one was wanted.

A substitute that stands in for something real is registered the same way, even
where no checker reads the file it lives in. The rule is about what was asked
and answered, not about what a scanner happens to recognise.

## B404 and B603, the subprocess in the Python suite

Asked 2026-08-28. Answered: allowed.

Two lines are allowed, not the module. Both pragmas are line-scoped, which
is how bandit applies them, so a second `subprocess.run` added anywhere in this
suite is reported and is not covered by anything here.

    python/tests/test_wrench.py:13    import subprocess
    python/tests/test_wrench.py:681   result = subprocess.run(

Those are the lines as of wrench `8d27c06`. A line number drifts, so the source
text is quoted beside it: if the number no longer names that text, this entry is
describing something that has moved and needs re-reading before it is trusted.

Both sit in `test_the_pack_is_importable_without_an_install`, and bandit reports
`B404 blacklist` for the import and
`B603 subprocess_without_shell_equals_true` for the call.

The question: `test_the_pack_is_importable_without_an_install` discharges
FR-6.1, which says the pack imports from a source checkout with no install
having run. The only way to observe that is a fresh interpreter, which means a
subprocess. Bandit is right that a subprocess is worth looking at and wrong that
this one is a risk: the argument vector is a literal, the executable is
`sys.executable`, no shell is involved, and nothing in it comes from outside the
test.

Config could not do it. The shared jig invokes `bandit -r -q --skip B101
tests` and passes no `-c`, so bandit never reads `[tool.bandit]` from
`pyproject.toml` and a configured skip would not be seen. A skip that did reach
it would also apply to the `security` task over the pack's own source, which
would weaken a check that currently passes on its merits.

Some things are deliberately not suppressed. `B101 assert_used` is skipped by the
jig itself, on its own reasoning, and no pragma here touches it. The `security`
task over `python/wrench/` carries no suppressions at all.

## Index

    python/tests/test_wrench.py   # nosec B404
    python/tests/test_wrench.py   # nosec B603

The paths are relative to the repository root, whichever base the checker is
scanning from. `common-quality` runs at each pack's own base, so a register
serving more than one of them cannot be written in any single scan's frame. The
checker resolves both sides against the repository, found by walking up for
`.git`, which is the frame a person writes in anyway.

That is a toolbox change, and it closes a defect in the shared register.

A row for a pack this scan does not cover is left alone, not reported gone.
That is the half that makes one register serve two bases: without it, a
scan at `python/` would call every Go row a pragma that had vanished. A row
naming a file that exists nowhere is still a phantom wherever it sits, because
no other scan would catch it either.

One row per pragma, not one per file. The checker keys on the set of codes a
single pragma carries, so the two lines above are two entries of one code each
rather than one entry carrying both. Registering them together reads as a third
pragma silencing `B404` and `B603` at once, which is not what the source has.

This register is checked from toolbox `6ac4304`, which taught
`bin/suppression-register.py` every language and not Go alone. Before it the
checker matched gosec's `G\d+` and not bandit's `B\d+`, so it passed vacuously
here. wrench symlinks the checker, so the upgrade reached this repository with
no change here, and the two rows above are spelled for the checker, not for a
reader.

## SLF001, the guard that no public entry point can reach

Asked 2026-09-04. Answered: allowed, one line.

    python/tests/test_json_canonical_refusals.py    # noqa: SLF001

`_Shipped.validate` raises when the name it was built with is not in the shipped
set. Nothing public can produce that: the four shipped instances are module
constants built from the generated set, so the name and the set agree by
construction. It is a guard against the set and the constants coming apart,
which is a programming error rather than an input.

The question: a guard nothing exercises is a guard whose message nobody has
read, and this one names the name precisely because "no shipped schema" with no
subject tells a reader nothing. Reaching the private class is the only way to
see it fire. The alternative was leaving `wrench/schema.py` at 77.8% of branches
against the 80% the gate enforces. That would have been a coverage failure
settled by not looking, which is exactly the case hard rule 5 covers.

One line, and it is line-scoped. A second private access anywhere in this
suite is reported and is covered by nothing here.

This entry retires if `_Shipped` gains a public constructor, which nothing
wants, or if the guard becomes unreachable and is deleted with its test.

## A replacement operator new that fails on demand, in the C++ suite

Asked 2026-09-16, of infobot, and again of wrench. Answered: yes.

    Q: The C++ pack will hit the same throw-edge gate. That approval was for
       infobot. Yes for wrench?
    A: yes

    cpp/tests/allocation.hpp
    cpp/tests/allocation.cpp

This is a substitute and not a pragma, so nothing silences a check and the
register's checker has no row to match: it reads `.go`, `.py`, `.rs`, `.rb` and
the shells, and neither of these files is any of those. It is here because a
test-only replacement of a global is the kind of thing that must be answered for
even when no tool asks.

What it does: `allocation.cpp` replaces the global `operator new` and
`operator delete` in the test binary alone. Every replacement allocates exactly
as the default does until a test arms a failure, and then the allocation it
names fails once. Nothing a consumer links carries the file, and nothing in the
library knows it exists.

Why it is needed. gcov counts the edge an allocating call takes when it throws,
and the C++ jig enforces 80% of branches per file. Nothing a test can hand the
pack makes an allocation fail, so those edges are unreachable from outside and
the file sits below the minimum with no defect in it. The two ways to make that
green without this are to pass `--exclude-throw-branches` or to stop gating
branches, and both are the case hard rule 5 covers: a coverage failure settled
by not looking.

It stands apart from a mock, which is why the answer was yes. A mock replaces
the thing under test with something that agrees with the test; this replaces the
allocator with one that behaves exactly as the real one does until it is told
otherwise, and what it then does, failing, is what the real one does under
memory pressure. The code under test is unchanged and unaware.

This entry retires if the pack stops being gated on branches, or if a way is
found to exhaust memory in a test without replacing the allocator, which on a
machine with overcommit there is not.
